[THOUGHT] The patient reports no additional psychosocial stressors beyond acute bereavement, and denies trauma history in this response. Social/exposure history (domain #6) is now reasonably addressed. Next, domain #7: targeted physical examination. Given the acute onset of command hallucinations after bereavement — with no clear substance, medication, or prior psychiatric history — I must prioritize ruling out medical mimics. A focused neurologic exam is essential: mental status (orientation, attention, thought process/form), cranial nerves (especially visual fields, fundoscopy for papilledema), cerebellar function, gait, and signs of meningeal irritation or focal deficits. Also, vital signs (fever, hypertension, tachycardia) and general appearance (e.g., agitation, psychomotor retardation, autonomic signs) are critical. Since I cannot perform the exam directly, I must request it as a structured clinical assessment.

[TEST] Perform a targeted physical examination including:  
- Vital signs (temperature, blood pressure, heart rate, respiratory rate);  
- Mental status exam (orientation to time/place/person, attention (e.g., digit span), thought process and content, insight/judgment);  
- Neurologic screening (cranial nerves II–XII, rapid alternating movements, gait, Romberg test, plantar responses);  
- General inspection for signs of acute illness (e.g., nuchal rigidity, rash, tremor, diaphoresis).

